= Resistor =

== Overview ==
A '''resistor''' is an electronic component designed to provide a specified electrical resistance in a circuit.

== Definition ==
Resistance relates voltage and current. In an ohmic component under appropriate conditions, Ohm's law expresses the relationship as V = IR, where V is voltage, I is current, and R is resistance.

== Scope & importance ==
Resistors make currents and voltage drops controllable. They are among the fundamental components used to bias, divide, limit, sense, terminate, and dissipate electrical energy in circuits.

== Core concepts ==
Important characteristics include resistance value, tolerance, power rating, temperature coefficient, noise, and construction type.

== Examples ==
A series resistor can limit current through an LED. Two resistors can form a voltage divider. A precision shunt resistor can be used to infer current from a measured voltage drop.

== Applications / implications ==
Resistors appear in analog and digital electronics, power systems, sensing, signal conditioning, timing networks, feedback networks, and circuit protection.

== Epistemic notes ==
The ideal resistor is a model. Real components have parasitic capacitance and inductance, finite power limits, temperature dependence, and manufacturing tolerance.

== Resistor, current, and power ==
[[Related to::Electric current|Electric current]] is the rate of electric charge flow through a cross-section. A resistor constrains the current–voltage relation and may convert electrical energy into heat. For an approximately ohmic resistor at stable temperature:

<pre>
V = I R
P = V I = I² R = V² / R
</pre>

Here V is the voltage across the component, I the current through it, R its resistance, and P the rate of electrical energy dissipation. The identities involving R assume the linear ohmic relation applies. A 100-ohm ideal resistor with 1 volt across it carries 0.01 ampere and dissipates 0.01 watt. A real device needs adequate power and temperature margins.

== Resistance versus resistivity ==
'''Resistance''' describes a particular component or conductor and is measured in ohms. '''Resistivity''' describes a material property and is measured in ohm-metres. In a uniform conductor model, resistance increases with length and decreases with cross-sectional area; real component geometry and construction can be more complicated.

== Practical design limitations ==
Resistor values drift with temperature and ageing. Parasitic capacitance/inductance, voltage limits, thermal noise, tolerance and packaging can matter at higher frequencies or precision requirements. A resistor is not inherently an ideal current limiter under all faults or supply conditions.

== Information-bearing circuit applications ==
A resistor is a passive electrical component. In signal circuits, the voltages and currents it establishes or measures can help encode electrical states. In a '''pull-up''' or '''pull-down''' arrangement, a resistor can bias an input toward a specified electrical state when another element is not driving it. Whether that state is interpreted as a digital 0 or 1 depends on the device's actual logic thresholds and noise margins.

In a '''voltage divider''', a resistor network produces a voltage related to an input or measured quantity. An analog-to-digital converter may subsequently sample that voltage; the resistor itself does not perform the conversion. A '''current-sense shunt''' similarly produces a voltage drop related to current that measurement circuitry can read.

Correct design depends on resistance tolerance, allowable dissipation, measurement loading, parasitic effects, temperature, noise and the receiving circuit's thresholds. A resistor therefore provides physical parameters and constraints in a signal pathway; its role depends on the surrounding circuit. See [[Voltage]], [[Electric current]] and [[Computer architecture]].

